\description{
The symmetric proportionality measure \eqn{\phi_s} of Quinn et al.
(2017), propr's \code{"phs"}: the variance of the log ratio of two features
relative to that of their log product. \code{0} means perfectly proportional.
}
\details{
With \eqn{z} the log-ratio-transformed abundances (clr or alr),
\deqn{\phi_{s,ij} = \frac{\mathrm{var}(z_i - z_j)}{\mathrm{var}(z_i +
z_j)} = \frac{1 - \rho_{p,ij}}{1 + \rho_{p,ij}}}
where \eqn{\mathrm{var}(z_i - z_j)} is \code{\link[=vlr]{vlr()}} and \eqn{\rho_p} is
\code{\link[=rho_p]{rho_p()}}. It lies in \eqn{[0, \infty)}: 0 for proportional features, 1
for unrelated ones, and infinite for reciprocal ones (\eqn{z_i = -z_j}).
It is computed as \eqn{\mathrm{vlr}_{ij} / (2\mathrm{var}(z_i) +
2\mathrm{var}(z_j) - \mathrm{vlr}_{ij})}, since \eqn{\mathrm{var}(a + b) =
2\mathrm{var}(a) + 2\mathrm{var}(b) - \mathrm{var}(a - b)}. Unlike
\code{\link[=vlr]{vlr()}}, it depends on the \code{denominator}. Near-reciprocal pairs (such as
the two features of a two-feature \code{denominator}, whose \eqn{z} are exact
opposites) have \eqn{\mathrm{var}(z_i + z_j)} near 0, where rounding
dominates: their value is \code{Inf} or merely very large.

Lovell et al.'s (2015) original \eqn{\phi = \mathrm{vlr}_{ij} /
\mathrm{var}(z_i)} is not symmetric (\eqn{\phi_{ij} \ne \phi_{ji}}), so
it can't be a \code{dist}; Quinn et al. (2017) replaced it with \eqn{\phi_s}.
This is the measure that propr computes with \code{propr(counts, metric = "phs")} (see \strong{Zeros} for its zero handling).
